1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
In 1916, Germany became the first country

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:05,360
to adopt daylight saving time

3
00:00:05,360 --> 00:00:07,760
nationwide, during wartime.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:09,840
America adopted it, repealed

5
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:13,360
it despite a presidential veto, then restored it.

6
00:00:13,360 --> 00:00:14,160
Why?

7
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:17,200
Earlier proposals and local trials existed.

8
00:00:17,200 --> 00:00:19,600
War accelerated adoption.

9
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:23,920
In 1918, America shifted clocks to save resources.

10
00:00:23,920 --> 00:00:26,720
After the war, savings were disputed.

11
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:29,120
Why did this trouble some farmers?

12
00:00:29,120 --> 00:00:30,320
Clocks shifted.

13
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:32,400
The sun and dew didn't.

14
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,440
At hearings, some complained:

15
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:37,040
hired workers waited for dew to dry;

16
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:40,400
stores in nearby towns closed an hour earlier.

17
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:42,160
Other objections arose.

18
00:00:42,160 --> 00:00:45,920
In 1919, Congress repealed federal seasonal clock

19
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:48,640
changes, overriding Wilson's veto.

20
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:51,920
In 1942, another war restored it:

21
00:00:51,920 --> 00:00:54,400
clocks advanced an hour year-round.

22
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:58,800
Separate legislation ended it September 30, 1945.

23
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,040
1966 standardized scheduling with state opt-outs.

24
00:01:03,040 --> 00:01:06,000
Today's dates began in 2007.

25
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:07,480
War didn't invent it;

26
00:01:07,480 --> 00:01:09,700
it accelerated its spread when...
